<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Yarp.ReverseProxy" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      MultipartInspectionReader, UploadInspectionService and PooledRequestBuffer are internal by
      design - they are gateway plumbing, not a surface community rule packages implement against.
      The multipart reader is also the component whose failure modes are least visible from the
      outside: a boundary the gateway refuses to parse, a limit that stops the read, a sample that
      is one byte short of a magic-byte signature. Testing those through HTTP only would mean
      inferring the status from a response code, so the test assembly is granted direct access
      instead, following the precedent WPShield.Rules.WordPress.csproj already sets.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="WPShield.Gateway.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../WPShield.Core/WPShield.Core.csproj" />
    <!--
      The reference that makes WPShield a security gateway rather than a reverse proxy with a size
      limit. Until M2 the Gateway could not see a single rule: the rule set executed only from the
      WPShield.Service console demonstration, so none of the detection this project documents ran on
      real traffic.

      The direction is one-way and it is an architectural invariant, not a preference. The Gateway
      may reference the Rules project; the Rules project may never reference the Gateway, because
      Abstractions, Core and Rules.WordPress must stay free of ASP.NET Core, YARP, IIS and
      Windows-only dependencies. A Linux CI leg builds and tests exactly those three projects, which
      is what keeps the claim falsifiable rather than aspirational.

      No PackageReference is needed for the multipart machinery. Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities
      and Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers ship in the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework, so
      MultipartReader, MediaTypeHeaderValue and HeaderUtilities are already available to this
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web project and Directory.Packages.props is untouched.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../WPShield.Rules.WordPress/WPShield.Rules.WordPress.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Update="appsettings.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!--
      Operator overlay holding real hostnames and destinations. It is ignored by git and
      never copied into a published artifact, so deployment packages cannot leak topology.
    -->
    <Content Update="appsettings.Local.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>Never</CopyToPublishDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
